\description{
\code{div_richness()} gives the non-parametric richness estimators of
EstimateS (Colwell 2013) and SpadeR (Chao et al. 2016) for every site,
with bootstrap standard errors and log-normal confidence intervals.

For abundance data (counts of individuals):
\itemize{
\item \code{chao1}: Chao (1984), with the small-sample factor (n - 1)/n.
\item \code{chao1_bc}: the bias-corrected Chao1, finite even without doubletons.
\item \code{ichao1}: the improved Chao1 of Chiu et al. (2014), which also uses
tripletons and quadrupletons.
\item \code{ace}, \code{ace1}: the abundance-based coverage estimators of Chao and Lee
(1992); species seen more than \code{k} times are "abundant" and counted as
observed.
\item \code{jack1}, \code{jack2}: first- and second-order jackknife (Burnham and
Overton 1978).
}

For incidence data (detections in sampling units, given with \code{unit}):
\itemize{
\item \code{chao2}, \code{chao2_bc}, \code{ichao2}: the incidence versions of the above.
\item \code{ice}, \code{ice1}: the incidence-based coverage estimators of Lee and Chao
(1994), with the improved coverage estimate of Chao et al. (2016).
\item \code{jack1}, \code{jack2}: jackknife estimators on sampling units.
\item \code{bootstrap}: the bootstrap estimator of Smith and van Belle (1984).
}

Point estimates equal those of SpadeR (and of vegan's \code{estimateR()} and
\code{specpool()} where they compute the same estimator); this is checked in
the test suite. Standard errors come from \code{nboot} bootstrap samples of
the estimated community, as in \code{\link[=div_estimate]{div_estimate()}}, so every estimator has
one.

Which to report? Chao1/Chao2 are lower bounds with good statistical
properties and are the usual choice; iChao1/iChao2 are less biased when
many species are rare. ACE/ICE and the jackknives are given for
comparison with older studies. For comparing sites, prefer
coverage-standardised diversity (\code{\link[=div_standardise]{div_standardise()}}).
